    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_a85f2a24ad9978597194ccc1f978f4c453c8f1ca155ebf2a175aad9149f527d2",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Across the introduction, exponential specialization, parameter experiment, and conclusion, the source shows that smoothing shape alone does not determine computational length: with an order-one incomplete-gamma parameter, asymptotic decrease of the correction terms constrains the scale K to order t, whereas an s-scaled parameter or logarithmic cutoff geometry can retain a square-root-length main sum. Within the order-t regime, larger K trades a longer main sum for greater attainable accuracy, while larger smoothing power p sharpens main-sum decay but shortens the useful correction series and can reduce total accuracy.",
